Step 8 Socratic Question: What should I check to avoid the mistake of circular logic, which is drawing conclusions in advance into assumptions? Pause and observe the step carefully: This is step 8, 8 pages. Since two right angles of 90 degrees meet and form a straight angle of 180 degrees, the large triangle becomes an isosceles triangle with two sides (hypotenuse H) of equal length. Due to the properties of isosceles triangles, the bisector of the vertex angle bisects the base, thus completing the principle of ASA congruence. What you must remember at this time is that in a regular triangle, two sides are not congruent even if the non-included angle (SSA) is the same, but in a right triangle (90 degrees), RHS congruence is always established thanks to the symmetry of isosceles triangles!

